To determine the total number of problems Katie has to complete, we need to consider both her math and reading homework.

---

### Step 1: Calculate the number of math problems

Katie has 7 pages of math homework, and each page contains 9 problems.

$$
7 \text{ pages} \times 9 \text{ problems per page} = 63 \text{ math problems}
$$

---

### Step 2: Calculate the number of reading problems

Katie also has 3 pages of reading homework, with each page containing 9 problems.

$$
3 \text{ pages} \times 9 \text{ problems per page} = 27 \text{ reading problems}
$$

---

### Step 3: Add the problems from math and reading

To find the total number of problems, we add the math and reading problems together:

$$
63 \text{ (math problems)} + 27 \text{ (reading problems)} = 90 \text{ total problems}
$$

---

### Alternative Check:

We can also think of this as total pages (7 math + 3 reading = 10 pages) with 9 problems per page:

$$
10 \text{ pages} \times 9 \text{ problems per page} = 90 \text{ total problems}
$$

Both methods confirm the same result.

---

### Final Answer:

$$
\boxed{90}
$$

Katie has to complete **90 problems** in total. 📚✅

$$
\boxed{90}
$$

